{"name":"Blue Fescue","hex":"#adb6ac","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"},{"term":"fescue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A straw, wire, stick, etc., used chiefly to point out letters to children when learning to read.","A hardy grass commonly used to border golf fairways in temperate climates, of the genus Festuca."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To use a fescue, or teach with a fescue."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fescue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Blue fescue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue fescue is a common name for several plant species and may refer to:"},{"items":["Festuca glauca","Festuca longifolia","Festuca pallens"]}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue_fescue"},{"title":"Festuca","blocks":[{"text":"Festuca (fescue) is a genus of flowering plants belonging to the grass family Poaceae. They are evergreen, herbaceous, or perennial, tufted grasses with a height range of 10–200 cm (4–79 in) and a cosmopolitan distribution, occurring on every continent except Antarctica. The genus is closely related to ryegrass (Lolium), and recent evidence from phylogenetic studies using DNA sequencing of plant mitochondrial DNA shows that the genus lacks monophyly. As a result, plant taxonomists have moved several species, including the forage grasses tall fescue and meadow fescue, from the genus Festuca into the genus Lolium or alternatively into the segregated genus Schedonorus."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Lolium-pratense1web.jpg/330px-Lolium-pratense1web.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Festuca"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Festuca_pratensis_%287902308284%29.jpg/500px-Festuca_pratensis_%287902308284%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Festuca_pratensis_%287902308284%29.jpg/1280px-Festuca_pratensis_%287902308284%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/76/Festuca_pratensis_%287902308284%29.jpg/500px-Festuca_pratensis_%287902308284%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Fescue grass, Montana","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFestuca_pratensis_(7902308284).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/2010._%D0%94%D0%BE%D0%BD%D0%B5%D1%86%D0%BA._%D0%9A%D0%B0%D1%80%D0%BD%D0%B0%D0%B2%D0%B0%D0%BB_%D0%BD%D0%B0_%D0%B4%D